00\">a</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br></span><span style=\"color:#660\">}</span></div></code></div><br><br>and <br><br><div style=\"background-color:rgb(250,250,250);border-color:rgb(187,187,187);border-style:solid;border-width:1px;word-wrap:break-word\"><code><div><span style=\"color:#000\">output$text1 </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> renderText</span><span style=\"color:#660\">({</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> getB</span><span style=\"color:#660\">()</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">})</span></div></code></div><br>I am not sure of the next step.  Does an image have to be saved locally, before being displayed?  Can it be displayed directly by giving the file path (e.g.  <a href=\"https://s3.amazonaws.com/bucketname/logo.gif\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://s3.amazonaws.com/bucketname/logo.gif&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792396571308000&amp;usg=AOvVaw1JE-J3qI-71_CgFkk0Dz3V\">https://s3.amazonaws.com/bucketname/logo.gif</a>)?  I have tried this but it does not work.<span><font color=\"#888888\"><br></font></span></div></blockquote></div></div>"
